---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About Us — NetSpeed Internet Telemetry & Diagnostics"
  description="Learn about the design, methodology, and engineering behind NetSpeed. Our speed test engine provides uncompromised telemetry via optimal server selection."
>
  <main class="flex-1 py-16 px-4 md:px-8">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-12">
      {/* Hero Header Block */}
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-b border-hairline pb-8">
        <span
          class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block"
        >
          Our Mission
        </span>
        <h1
          class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tighter text-ink font-sans leading-none"
        >
          Engineered for pure network transparency.
        </h1>
        <p
          class="text-base md:text-lg text-body max-w-3xl leading-relaxed mt-2"
        >
          NetSpeed was founded with a singular objective: to provide developers,
          network administrators, and everyday users with an accurate, ads-free,
          and high-fidelity telemetry platform. By deploying concurrent streams
          and measuring real-time latency variance, we expose the true
          limitations of your internet routing.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* Core Technology Philosophy Grid */}
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 pt-4">
        <div class="vercel-card p-6 flex flex-col gap-3 justify-between">
          <div>
            <span
              class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block mb-1"
            >
              Methodology 01
            </span>
              <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
              Zero payload caching
            </h2>
            <p class="text-xs md:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed mt-2">
              Traditional speed test applications fetch static files that can
              easily be cached by local internet service providers (ISPs) or
              content delivery networks (CDNs). NetSpeed leverages Cloudflare's
              edge network to serve non-compressible binary payloads directly
              from the nearest data center, forcing your ISP to handle raw,
              unoptimized data transit.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="vercel-card p-6 flex flex-col gap-3 justify-between">
          <div>
            <span
              class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block mb-1"
            >
              Methodology 02
            </span>
              <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
              Concurrent socket piping
            </h2>
            <p class="text-xs md:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed mt-2">
              Using Web Workers, we initiate six concurrent download connections
              and adaptive upload streams. This parallel pipe design stresses
              your connection capacity, allowing us to accurately measure the
              peak saturation performance and load-balanced capabilities of your
              network interface.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="vercel-card p-6 flex flex-col gap-3 justify-between">
          <div>
            <span
              class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block mb-1"
            >
              Methodology 03
            </span>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
              Bufferbloat loaded latency
            </h2>
            <p class="text-xs md:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed mt-2">
              A high download speed is useless if your ping spikes to several
              hundred milliseconds when downloading files. We run loaded latency
              tests during both download and upload stages to quantify
              bufferbloat stability and let you know if your router's queueing
              algorithm is failing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="vercel-card p-6 flex flex-col gap-3 justify-between">
          <div>
            <span
              class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block mb-1"
            >
              Methodology 04
            </span>
              <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
              Optimal server selection
            </h2>
            <p class="text-xs md:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed mt-2">
              NetSpeed identifies which Cloudflare edge datacenter is serving
              your request. Cloudflare's Anycast BGP routing automatically
              connects you to the nearest edge based on real-time network
              topology. We display the selected edge and estimated distance for
              transparency, ensuring you know exactly where your measurements
              are being taken.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Why NetSpeed Exists */}
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block"
          >
            Background
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Why NetSpeed exists.
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
            Most speed test tools have a blind spot. They download a static file
            from a server, measure how fast it arrives, and report a number. The
            problem is that ISPs and content delivery networks can cache those
            files locally. When that happens, the test measures the speed from the
            cache to your device — not from the internet to your device. The
            result looks good on paper, but it does not reflect what you actually
            experience when streaming, gaming, or working from home.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
            NetSpeed was built to solve this. Every test uses randomly generated
            binary data that cannot be cached or compressed. The data is served
            from Cloudflare's nearest edge data center, and six parallel streams
            stress the connection to find its real ceiling. The engine also
            measures loaded latency — your ping while the network is under heavy
            load — which reveals bufferbloat that most tools ignore entirely. The
            goal is not to show you the biggest possible number. It is to show
            you the truth about your connection.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Who Built This */}
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block"
          >
            The team
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Who built this.
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
            NetSpeed is built and maintained by a single developer based in
            Assam, India. It started as a side project to understand how network
            measurement actually works and grew into a tool that people around
            the world use to diagnose their connections. The entire codebase is
            open source — you can inspect it, contribute to it, or fork it for
            your own use.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-2">
            <a
              href="https://github.com/xvistasss/netspeed"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-canvas text-ink border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong font-medium text-xs rounded-full py-2 px-5 transition-[border-color] cursor-pointer select-none"
            >
              <svg
                class="w-3.5 h-3.5"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
              >
                <path
                  d="M9 19c-5 1.5-5-2.5-7-3m14 6v-3.87a3.37 3.37 0 0 0-.94-2.61c3.14-.35 6.44-1.54 6.44-7A5.44 5.44 0 0 0 20 4.77 5.07 5.07 0 0 0 19.91 1S18.73.65 16 2.48a13.38 13.38 0 0 0-7 0C6.27.65 5.09 1 5.09 1A5.07 5.07 0 0 0 5 4.77a5.44 5.44 0 0 0-1.5 3.78c0 5.42 3.3 6.61 6.44 7A3.37 3.37 0 0 0 9 18.13V22"
                ></path>
              </svg>
              View on GitHub
            </a>
            <a
              href="/contact"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-canvas text-ink border border-hairline hover:border-hairline-strong font-medium text-xs rounded-full py-2 px-5 transition-[border-color] cursor-pointer select-none"
            >
              Get in touch
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* How It Differs */}
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block"
          >
            Comparison
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            How it differs from other speed tests.
          </h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mt-2">
            <div class="vercel-card p-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
                Non-compressible payloads
              </h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
                Traditional tools download static files that ISPs can cache
                locally. NetSpeed generates unique binary data on every test,
                forcing your ISP to handle real internet traffic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="vercel-card p-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
                Six parallel streams
              </h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
                A single download connection often cannot saturate your
                connection. Six Web Worker streams running simultaneously find
                the real ceiling of your bandwidth.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="vercel-card p-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
                Loaded latency measurement
              </h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
                Most tools only measure idle ping. NetSpeed measures latency
                while the network is under heavy load, revealing bufferbloat
                that idle tests miss.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="vercel-card p-5 flex flex-col gap-2">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
                Cloudflare edge routing
              </h3>
              <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
                Anycast BGP routing connects you to the nearest Cloudflare edge
                automatically. No manual server selection, no misleading
                results from distant servers.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Open Source & Transparency */}
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <span
            class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-mute block"
          >
            Open source
          </span>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink font-sans">
            Open source and transparent.
          </h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
            The entire NetSpeed codebase is publicly available on GitHub. You can
            see exactly how every measurement is calculated, how the server
            selection works, and how the quality score is derived. There are no
            hidden trackers, no data selling, and no dark patterns. If you find a
            bug, want to contribute a feature, or just want to understand how
            network measurement works, the code is there for you to explore.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
            Transparency is not just a feature — it is the foundation. When you
            run a speed test, you deserve to know how the result was calculated
            and what it actually means. NetSpeed shows you every metric, every
            server detail, and every raw measurement so you can draw your own
            conclusions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* CTA Banner */}
      <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-12">
        <div
          class="flex flex-col md:flex-row items-start md:items-center justify-between gap-6 bg-canvas-soft-2 p-8 rounded-lg border border-hairline"
        >
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-xl">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
              Ready to see your real numbers?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
              Run a detailed speed test to measure your download, upload,
              latency, jitter, and bufferbloat. Every metric is explained so
              you know exactly what the results mean.
            </p>
          </div>
          <a
            href="/"
            class="bg-primary text-on-primary font-medium text-xs rounded-full py-2 px-6 hover:opacity-90 active:scale-95 transition-[opacity,transform] text-center select-none cursor-pointer inline-block whitespace-nowrap shadow-xs"
          >
            Run Speed Test
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
